#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""earthnet-omarchy: data sidecar for the Earthnet Omarchy shell plugin.

The Quickshell plugin (QML) owns all drawing. This process owns everything
that needs the kernel or the network: connection capture, GeoIP, the land
geometry, the home location and the Omarchy theme palette.

It talks a tiny line-delimited JSON protocol on stdout:

  * one ``{"type":"init", ...}`` object at startup carrying home coordinates,
    the theme palette, land geometry and render hints;
  * one ``{"type":"frame", ...}`` object on every poll with the live trace
    list (destination coords, colour, label, protocol/port/state, age);
  * one ``{"type":"error", ...}`` line if a backend fails. Errors are
    non-fatal: the loop keeps retrying.

Commands (lines on stdin) let QML nudge the process cheaply:

  ``mode live|demo``   switch data source (demo = bundled sample traces)
  ``poll``             request an immediate frame
  ``demo`` / ``live``  aliases for ``mode``
  ``refresh``          reload theme + land and re-emit ``init``
  ``quit``             exit cleanly

The module reuses the already-installed ``earthnet`` package when it can find
it (``python3 -m earthnet``), and otherwise falls back to the copies under
``plugin_dir/pylib/earthnet``. That keeps the sidecar usable both from the
Earthnet checkout and from a standalone plugin install.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

